From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 23:27:39 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: keep the line input IRQ registered across a tty throttle

line_throttle() deactivates the input channel, which removes its
descriptor from the host event set and clears the read registration.
Nothing registers it again: commit 940b241d9050 ("um: Remove obsolete
reenable_XX calls") dropped the reactivation from line_unthrottle(),
which now drains the channel once and stops. n_tty throttles once 3968
bytes are unread, so a single larger burst to a serial line or the
console leaves its input dead for good.

Only record the throttle. While throttled, chan_interrupt() still fills
the flip buffer up to its memory limit and leaves the rest in the host
channel, the delayed retry does nothing, and line_unthrottle() drains
the backlog directly, which an edge-triggered descriptor requires.
Returning early from chan_interrupt() instead would push backpressure
into the host channel at the line-discipline limit. deactivate_chan()
has no other caller and goes.

Drain a line's input from one caller at a time.  The input IRQ,
line_unthrottle() and the retry work all call chan_interrupt(), from any
CPU, and the IRQ can interrupt a drain on its own CPU.  With the input IRQ
registered while throttled, an unthrottle drain on the reader's CPU
overlaps an IRQ drain elsewhere, and the two split and reorder the input
they read into a flip buffer that takes one producer at a time.  The first
caller now drains; a concurrent caller only records that input may be
waiting, and the drainer reads again until no record is left.
---
 arch/um/drivers/chan.h      |  1 -
 arch/um/drivers/chan_kern.c | 39 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 arch/um/drivers/line.c      |  8 +++++++-
 arch/um/drivers/line.h      |  2 ++
 4 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/chan.h b/arch/um/drivers/chan.h
index 5a61db512ffbf40fc889b0acfe8d1aed3ad99409..7296ee02a34415e318c4c89afea55859adf50c07 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/chan.h
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/chan.h
@@ -36,7 +36,6 @@ extern int write_chan(struct chan *chan, const u8 *buf, size_t len,
 extern int console_write_chan(struct chan *chan, const char *buf, 
 			      int len);
 extern int console_open_chan(struct line *line, struct console *co);
-extern void deactivate_chan(struct chan *chan, int irq);
 extern void chan_enable_winch(struct chan *chan, struct tty_port *port);
 extern int enable_chan(struct line *line);
 extern void close_chan(struct line *line);
diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/chan_kern.c b/arch/um/drivers/chan_kern.c
index 26442db7d60895c53392c6c73be203862118488c..be683af0648c2416789af63b00b86c38ed522e77 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/chan_kern.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/chan_kern.c
@@ -281,12 +281,6 @@ void close_chan(struct line *line)
 	}
 }
 
-void deactivate_chan(struct chan *chan, int irq)
-{
-	if (chan && chan->enabled)
-		deactivate_fd(chan->fd_in, irq);
-}
-
 int write_chan(struct chan *chan, const u8 *buf, size_t len, int write_irq)
 {
 	int n, ret = 0;
@@ -575,7 +569,7 @@ int parse_chan_pair(char *str, struct line *line, int device,
 	return 0;
 }
 
-void chan_interrupt(struct line *line, int irq)
+static void drain_chan(struct line *line)
 {
 	struct tty_port *port = &line->port;
 	struct chan *chan = line->chan_in;
@@ -608,3 +602,34 @@ void chan_interrupt(struct line *line, int irq)
  out:
 	tty_flip_buffer_push(port);
 }
+
+#define LINE_INPUT_DRAINING 0
+#define LINE_INPUT_PENDING 1
+
+/*
+ * The input IRQ, line_unthrottle() and the retry work all drain the input
+ * channel, from any CPU, and the IRQ can also interrupt a drain on its own
+ * CPU. The flip buffer takes one producer at a time, and two drains would
+ * also split and reorder the input they read. So the first caller drains,
+ * and a caller that finds a drain in progress only records that more input
+ * may be waiting. The drainer reads again until no record is left, then
+ * checks once more after it lets go. The host descriptor is edge-triggered,
+ * so an IRQ's record can be the only notice of new input; it is never lost.
+ */
+void chan_interrupt(struct line *line, int irq)
+{
+	set_bit(LINE_INPUT_PENDING, &line->input_state);
+	/*
+	 * Pairs with the barrier after the drainer lets go: either the drainer
+	 * sees this record, or this caller sees that the drain has ended.
+	 */
+	smp_mb__after_atomic();
+	while (!test_and_set_bit_lock(LINE_INPUT_DRAINING, &line->input_state)) {
+		while (test_and_clear_bit(LINE_INPUT_PENDING, &line->input_state))
+			drain_chan(line);
+		clear_bit_unlock(LINE_INPUT_DRAINING, &line->input_state);
+		smp_mb__after_atomic();
+		if (!test_bit(LINE_INPUT_PENDING, &line->input_state))
+			break;
+	}
+}
diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/line.c b/arch/um/drivers/line.c
index 43d8959cc746fb39e0fc3391b8db4d0f3d07724f..7936f500e0b29c99724b47f759d9d745abc5f3c9 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/line.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/line.c
@@ -215,7 +215,13 @@ void line_throttle(struct tty_struct *tty)
 {
 	struct line *line = tty->driver_data;
 
-	deactivate_chan(line->chan_in, line->read_irq);
+	/*
+	 * Only record the throttle. Deactivating the input descriptor would
+	 * remove it from the host event set, and nothing adds it back, so
+	 * input would stop for good. While throttled, chan_interrupt() fills
+	 * the flip buffer only up to its memory limit, the delayed retry does
+	 * nothing, and line_unthrottle() drains what the host still holds.
+	 */
 	line->throttled = 1;
 }
 
diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/line.h b/arch/um/drivers/line.h
index e8bd6f3dfb507771e1d7f9e89252904e14240199..899b54b5d48cc917677ffc34d970dc89d9701d88 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/line.h
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/line.h
@@ -42,6 +42,8 @@ struct line {
 	/*This lock is actually, mostly, local to*/
 	spinlock_t lock;
 	int throttled;
+	/* LINE_INPUT_* bits: chan_interrupt() drains from one caller at a time */
+	unsigned long input_state;
 	/* Yes, this is a real circular buffer.
 	 * XXX: And this should become a struct kfifo!
 	 *
